      "requestAttempts": 0,
      "pronunciationText": "In the percentage video, heating drove the water out of blue copper sulfate crystals, leaving a white powder. That's an observation, something we notice with our senses or instruments. To investigate it, we turn it into an inquiry question, one that data can answer. What happens when you heat crystals? That's too broad, because there's nothing specific to measure. A better question is: what percentage of the mass of blue copper sulfate crystals is lost when they're heated until their mass stops changing? It names what we measure, the mass before and after, and the condition, heating to a steady mass.",
      "humanListening": "pending"
    },
