        }
      ],
      "requestAttempts": 1,
      "pronunciationText": "Primary data are measurements you collect yourself, like masses read from a balance. Secondary data were collected by someone else, like the relative atomic masses behind our thirty-six point one percent, from a published data sheet. And some questions should only be answered with secondary data. At what temperature does copper sulfate itself start to break down? Testing that would mean very strong heating, which gives off harmful gases, so we'd look it up in published data from a reliable source.",
      "humanListening": "pending"
