        }
      ],
      "requestAttempts": 0,
      "pronunciationText": "Published data agree. Copper sulfate breaks down only when it's much hotter than it needs to be to lose its water. So we modify the hypothesis, with a condition and a better reason. If the crystals are heated gently until their mass stops changing, then they'll lose thirty-six point one percent of their mass, because gentle heat drives off the water, while strong heat also breaks down the copper sulfate. And Group B's evidence gives us a sharper question. How does the time of gentle heating affect the percentage of mass lost? Our prediction: the percentage rises, then levels off at thirty-six point one, once all the water has gone.",
      "humanListening": "pending"
